dsv41 patch 0042 (19.10.2026): exl3_mrow for 17..32 rows (verify windows of 3-4 streams).
Base: the recipe's plugin vllm_exl3_moe (cc59c36): csrc/exl3_mrow.cu as in the image, dense.py incl. our 0035.
0042b rewrites the 17..32-row kernel against the same base; apply 0042 or 0042b, not both.
Why: exl3_mrow takes at most 16 rows. 3 / 4 streams at k = 5 verify 18 / 24 rows, and above 16 rows every dense layer
ran through exllamav3 (fp16 cast, fp32 output, cat, cast back; wo_a with a copy per head group). Dense time per round
(micro-benchmark, one rank, 43 layers incl. the drafter): 15.0 ms at 16 rows, 20.7 / 21.0 ms at 18 / 24 rows.
How: a second work plan g32 per registered group (own k-slices, capped so that 32 input rows plus the epilogue fit the
opt-in shared memory, ~99 KB on GB10 -> ks <= 1280 at K5; own workspace and counters). The kernel takes "int MT"
instead of "bool M16" (0: 1-8, 1: 9-16, 2: 17-32 rows); MT=2 runs rows 16-31 as a second m16 tile on the same decoded
trellis fragments (weights read and decoded once, 8 instead of 4 mma.sync per double k-row).
Exactness: <= 16 rows take the old plan and kernel path (bit-identical). 17-32 rows are deterministic and row-invariant
within the 32-row plan but sum in a different fp32 order than the exllamav3 path, so texts under 3-4 streams can
diverge from some token on (same distribution).
Measured (GB10, 2000 MHz): GPU test 15/15 (reference, row invariance, graph replay); dense per round 18.4 / 19.6 ms at
18 / 24 rows. Serve at T = 1 with k = 5 at every batch size: prose 3 / 4 streams +3.9 / +2.5 % decode, below our gate.
Together with 0040 variant B (k = 4 at 3-4 streams, <= 20 rows), 0045 and 0046: see patches/0040.
Switch: VLLM_EXL3_MROW_MAX_ROWS=32. Default 16 = previous behaviour (the 32-row plan is registered but not used).
JIT: the new source has a new hash, so the plugin rebuilds exl3_mrow on first use, i.e. while the model loads. Build it
before the first start: python3 -c 'from vllm_exl3_moe import mrow; mrow.ext()' (README, quick start step 4).

